// A11, the aliasing case — decided rather than deferred.
//
// An interface object borrows storage, so `a` and `b` are two more names for `c`. That is new:
// before A11 nothing could be WRITTEN through either of them, so one value with three names was
// only ever read three ways.
//
// It is allowed, and the reason is that a second name for one object is already sound in Burxt.
// There is no concurrency; a class instance never moves; and every path here writes the same
// field slots `c.bump()` writes, through the same pointer, so the three names cannot disagree
// about what the value is. The counter goes 1, 2, 3 whichever name asks.
//
// What aliasing WOULD break is a rule that reasons about one name while the bytes belong to
// another — a growth attributed to `a` when it lands in `c`. That is not left to luck: the
// checker remembers which binding each interface object borrows (`dyn_source`) and the escape
// rules are asked about THAT name. See `tests/fail/a_dynamic_grows_an_outer_binding.bx`, where
// the refusal names `bag` and the reader only ever wrote `d`.
interface Bump { function bump(mutable self) -> Int }

class Cell { n: Int }

implement Bump for Cell {
    function (mutable self) bump() -> Int {
        self.n = self.n + 1;
        return self.n;
    }
}

let mutable c: Cell = Cell { n: 0 };
let mutable a: dynamic Bump = c;
let mutable b: dynamic Bump = c;
print(a.bump());
print(b.bump());
print(c.bump());
print(c.n);

// An immutable interface object over the same mutable value is still legal and still cannot
// write: the permission is on the name, and this name does not have it.
let watcher: dynamic Bump = c;
print(c.n);
